BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 50 780 Td (Willowbark eases fever and aches. Strip the inner bark of a young white willow) Tj T* (in spring, dry it in the shade, and steep a thumb's length of it in hot water) Tj T* (for a quarter hour. Drink a cup at dawn and another at dusk. Too much upsets the) Tj T* (stomach.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Comfrey, which the old wives call knitbone, is bound over a broken limb as a) Tj T* (poultice. Mash the fresh leaves with a little warm water, spread them on linen) Tj T* (and wrap the limb. Change it every second day. It mends bones faster than rest) Tj T* (alone, but never give it to drink.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Nightshade grows in the damp shade behind the mill. Its berries are black and) Tj T* (glossy and taste sweet, and a handful will stop the heart of a grown man.) Tj T* (Children must be kept away from the patch. If someone has eaten them, make them) Tj T* (vomit at once and send for the priest.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Yarrow stops bleeding. Press the crushed leaves into a fresh cut and hold them) Tj T* (there until the blood slows. Soldiers carry it dried in their pouches. A tea of) Tj T* (the flowers also brings out a sweat and breaks a fever, though it is weaker than) Tj T* (willowbark.) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
