BT /F1 11 Tf 14 TL 50 780 Td (Chamomile calms the nerves and brings sleep. Gather the small daisy-like flowers) Tj T* (in high summer, dry them on a cloth, and steep a spoonful in hot water before) Tj T* (bed. It is gentle enough for children with colic and old men who cannot sleep.) Tj T* () Tj T* (Mandrake root is sold at fairs as a love charm, but it is a poison that brings) Tj T* (visions and madness. The Herbalist's Guild forbids its sale. Any member found) Tj T* (selling mandrake loses their licence and pays a fine of fifty crowns.) Tj T* () Tj T* (On the keeping of herbs: store dried leaves in clay jars with tight lids, away) Tj T* (from sunlight and damp. Roots keep for two years, leaves for one, and flowers) Tj T* (lose their strength after a single winter. Label every jar with the plant and) Tj T* (the month it was gathered.) Tj T* () Tj T* (The road from Millbrook to Eastvale crosses the river at the old stone bridge.) Tj T* (Travellers should carry bread for two days and a good cloak, for the hills are) Tj T* (cold even in summer. The inn at the crossing charges three coppers a night.) Tj T* () Tj T* ET
